Quick Battle match timing

Change Quick Battle so its normal match clock begins at exactly 7:00 / 420 seconds.

Do not simply change the current shared CONFIG.battle.roundSeconds in a way that unintentionally changes Watch Mode. The existing Battle class serves both quick-battle and watch, so make match duration explicitly mode-aware. Keep Watch Mode at its current 5:00 behavior unless some shared refactor is required internally.

Quick Battle continues to be first to 3 points. Reaching the configured pointsToWin still ends the match immediately with the normal K.O. flow, including during overtime.

At the end of the normal 7:00 clock:

If one side has more points, do not start overtime. Finish the match through the normal time-up/result flow and award the win to the side with more points.

If the points are tied, do not use Launch Point yet and do not restart a draw. Enter a dedicated 60-second overtime period.

Overtime must be a real match state rather than resetting or starting a new battle. Keep the existing score, both fighters’ current Launch Points, Energy, cooldowns, positions, projectiles, summons, techniques, respawn state and all other live combat state. The fight simply continues under overtime rules.

The overtime clock starts at 1:00 and counts down to 0 using the same simulation-time semantics as the normal battle clock. Pause must continue to freeze the game exactly as it currently does.

At the end of overtime, determine the result in this order: first compare points; the side with more points wins. If points are still tied, compare the fighters’ current Launch Point values—the number already shown on the HUD. The fighter with the lower Launch Point wins. Preserve the existing epsilon-safe Launch Point comparison. If points and Launch Point are both equal, preserve Alva’s existing draw behavior: no result dialog, restart into a fresh match.

A fighter reaching 3 points during overtime still ends the match immediately as a normal Void/K.O. victory instead of waiting for the overtime clock to expire.

Represent overtime explicitly in Battle rather than overloading unrelated states. A clean implementation may use an overtime phase or an explicit match-period/state field while keeping combat live. Fighters must remain controllable throughout overtime; unlike timeup or ko, overtime is active gameplay.

Restart and rematch must completely clear overtime state and restore the normal starting Void, normal wave speed, 0–0 score and the correct 7:00 Quick Battle clock.

