Overtime Void pressure

During the normal 7:00 fight, the Void behaves exactly as it does now.

The overtime pressure begins at the instant overtime starts and increases continuously throughout the 60 seconds.

The Void’s animated waves must become progressively faster as overtime advances. The current Void wave definitions live in js/stages/stage-theme.js as VOID.waves. Keep their existing organic shape, amplitudes, weights, red rim, black fill and general visual identity; increase their animation rate rather than changing them into a different effect.

Use an overtime progress value clamped from 0 to 1. The wave-speed multiplier should increase smoothly and monotonically from normal speed at overtime start to approximately 4× normal speed at the end of overtime. Prefer a progressive/ease-in curve such as 1 + 3 * progress² so the acceleration becomes increasingly noticeable rather than jumping immediately.

Respect the existing reduced-motion setting. With reduced motion enabled, the wave oscillation should remain still as it does now. The gameplay boundary movement described below still occurs because it affects gameplay, not merely presentation.

At the same time, the actual Void must gradually close in on the arena from the left, right and bottom only.

Do not move the top Void boundary at all.

This must not be a visual fake. The rendered Void edge and the actual StageCollision.inVoid kill boundary must use the same current bounds so a fighter is eliminated exactly where the displayed hazard says it should be. Projectiles and any other systems that use stage.inVoid should naturally respect the new bounds as well.

StageCollision currently freezes a copy of map.voidBounds. Refactor it cleanly so a Battle can update its active Void bounds while keeping the map’s original bounds immutable. Preserve the original bounds as the reset/baseline values.

Over the 60 seconds, linearly bring the left/right boundaries inward and the bottom boundary upward. Centralize the tuning in CONFIG, not in the renderer. Use conservative final clearances approximately equivalent to:

sideEndGap: 120 world units beyond each main-stage ledge.

bottomEndGap: 140 world units below mainStage.top.

These are minimum final gaps, not absolute world coordinates. Calculate the destination relative to each map’s mainStage, making the system stage-independent.

Therefore at 100% overtime progress:

left = mainStage.left - sideEndGap

right = mainStage.right + sideEndGap

bottom = mainStage.top + bottomEndGap

top = originalVoid.top

Interpolate from each stage’s original voidBounds to those destinations over the complete 60 seconds.

The left and right Void must never touch or cross the playable stage’s ledges, and the bottom Void must never reach or cross the main-stage surface. Clamp defensively even if future stage data changes.

Do not change mainStage, platforms, solids, camera physics or spawn points. The arena itself is not shrinking; only the Void kill region is moving closer.

The existing camera bounds may stay based on the stage’s original Void envelope. Do not make the camera suddenly snap inward with the overtime boundary.

A fighter who remains outside a newly advanced Void boundary as it closes in should be eliminated normally on the next fixed simulation step. This should use the same shared Void flow as any other elimination.

Ensure the stage renderer uses the active Void bounds instead of continuing to read the original map.voidBounds. Avoid keeping an independent copy in the renderer that can drift out of sync with collision.

Fighter-colored elimination explosion

Whenever a fighter is eliminated by entering the Void, add a short procedural explosion/burst at that fighter’s final world position immediately before the fighter disappears.

This is a visual effect only. It must not damage, launch, stun or otherwise affect either fighter.

The effect should fit Alva’s existing Canvas style: crisp, readable, short-lived and not excessively flashy. Use a radial burst with approximately 20–30 particles/shards plus a quick expanding central flash or ring. It should last roughly 0.45–0.65 seconds, expand rapidly, then fade cleanly. Scale it relative to the fighter rather than using one enormous screen-space effect.

The explosion’s colors must come from the eliminated fighter’s own color palette.

Do not implement this as if fighter.id === '0001' / if fighter.id === '0002' inside the renderer. Add reusable fighter-owned visual data, preferably something like visual.eliminationPalette, containing several representative colors for that fighter. Add appropriate palettes to the existing fighter definition modules after inspecting their sprite art. Use roughly 3–5 recognizable colors per fighter and provide a reasonable neutral fallback palette for future fighters that have not specified one.

Keep palette ownership in character data and effect behavior in the shared rendering system.

Trigger the effect in the shared Void/elimination path so it works consistently anywhere a real fighter is taken by the Void, including simultaneous eliminations. Capture the fighter’s location and palette before it is removed from inPlay.

The effect should continue drawing after the fighter has disappeared. Do not depend on the eliminated fighter continuing to render.

Make the burst deterministic enough for automated tests and consistent with the current deterministic procedural hit effects. Do not use uncontrolled gameplay randomness.

Reduced motion should remove or greatly reduce any screen shake/large movement associated with this effect, but a short color burst/fade may remain so elimination still has visual feedback.

Do not add gore or character-specific death animations. This is a clean energy-like explosion effect.

